WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.459 --> 00:00:05.710
(In Ilnu-aimun: Hello).

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:06.878
I’m Valérie Courtois

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:09.339
and I’m from the Ilnu community
of Mashteuiatsh,

00:00:09.381 --> 00:00:12.050
located in Pekuakami or Lac Saint-Jean,

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:14.261
the heart of what is now known as Québec.

00:00:15.095 --> 00:00:17.889
It is an honor to be here
with you tonight.

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:22.561
A good friend and colleague of mine,
the Honorable Ethel Blondin-Andrew,

00:00:22.561 --> 00:00:26.022
the first Indigenous woman
to be elected to Canada's parliament

00:00:26.022 --> 00:00:28.066
and to serve in cabinet,

00:00:28.108 --> 00:00:30.277
shared with me some wisdom she received:

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:31.987
“A mark of a good leader

00:00:32.028 --> 00:00:34.447
is if they leave a room
filled with more hope

00:00:34.489 --> 00:00:36.074
than when they first arrived.”

00:00:36.825 --> 00:00:40.412
I'm here with you tonight
to attempt to fulfill that aspiration.

00:00:40.745 --> 00:00:42.414
To leave you with hope.

00:00:43.164 --> 00:00:45.041
We need it now more than ever.

00:00:45.041 --> 00:00:47.794
Our home, our shared beautiful mother,

00:00:47.836 --> 00:00:51.172
our planet is experiencing
ecological turmoil.

00:00:51.214 --> 00:00:54.050
We humans are transforming it to a point

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:58.388
where we are risking the survival
of millions of species.

00:00:58.388 --> 00:01:02.892
And as a result, our societies
are also experiencing a parallel turmoil

00:01:02.892 --> 00:01:05.604
as we struggle to adapt
to the unnatural pace

00:01:05.645 --> 00:01:07.022
and scale of change.

00:01:07.814 --> 00:01:13.069
My homelands, known as Nitassinan,
are also experiencing these changes.

00:01:13.612 --> 00:01:17.407
It is a harsh, but strikingly beautiful
part of the boreal forest.

00:01:17.782 --> 00:01:21.119
Nitassinan is at its best when it is cold.

00:01:21.786 --> 00:01:27.292
Yet, we are seeing extreme changes in ice
and in key species such as caribou.

00:01:27.584 --> 00:01:30.670
When I first drove into Labrador
two decades ago,

00:01:30.712 --> 00:01:33.590
I had to stop on the 
Trans-Labrador Highway for hours

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:36.134
as the George River caribou herd crossed.

00:01:36.718 --> 00:01:40.013
There were hundreds
of thousands in the herd then.

00:01:40.555 --> 00:01:43.600
Now, there are only 8,000 left.

00:01:44.559 --> 00:01:47.520
I've seen firsthand the devastating
impact of climate change

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:50.982
and the loss of biodiversity
in my homeland of Nitassinan

00:01:50.982 --> 00:01:54.110
and all across what is now
known as Canada.

00:01:55.278 --> 00:01:58.031
But I’ve also seen something else,

00:01:58.406 --> 00:02:00.909
something that gives me hope.

00:02:01.743 --> 00:02:03.828
It’s not a technology from a lab.

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:06.498
It’s not a policy made in Ottawa or DC.

00:02:07.082 --> 00:02:11.044
It is the fundamental understanding
that is expressed by our knowledge,

00:02:11.044 --> 00:02:14.089
by our elders and knowledge
keepers this way:

00:02:14.089 --> 00:02:18.468
If we take care of the land,
the land takes care of us.

00:02:19.678 --> 00:02:21.304
Let me say it again.

00:02:21.304 --> 00:02:25.684
If we take care of the land,
the land takes care of us.

00:02:26.184 --> 00:02:28.812
This is about a relationship,

00:02:28.853 --> 00:02:30.438
a mutual love story.

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:37.320
(Applause)

00:02:38.029 --> 00:02:41.533
It's not an accident that 80 percent
of the world's remaining biodiversity

00:02:41.533 --> 00:02:46.413
are located on lands managed and loved
by Indigenous peoples.

00:02:46.454 --> 00:02:49.165
We have been in relationships
with the plants and animals

00:02:49.165 --> 00:02:51.710
of our territories
and waters for millennia.

00:02:52.377 --> 00:02:54.003
We care for each other.

00:02:54.629 --> 00:02:57.006
The Innu people have loved and sustained

00:02:57.006 --> 00:03:01.177
and been in harmony with our landscapes
for nearly 10,000 years.

00:03:01.636 --> 00:03:06.349
Surely we have values, insights,
strategies and knowledge to offer

00:03:06.391 --> 00:03:10.311
to the rest of the global community
with respect to how to be a part of

00:03:10.311 --> 00:03:12.105
and care for our environment.

00:03:12.897 --> 00:03:15.191
This knowledge is essential right now.

00:03:15.692 --> 00:03:21.614
It can help people and the land heal
from ecological crises and colonization.

00:03:22.115 --> 00:03:24.117
It can help restore the planet

00:03:24.117 --> 00:03:25.910
and it can help save us all.

00:03:26.578 --> 00:03:30.707
By fully respecting and acknowledging
Indigenous-led approaches to the land,

00:03:30.749 --> 00:03:33.918
we can help create
a better future for all.

00:03:34.794 --> 00:03:36.379
What does it look like?

00:03:36.421 --> 00:03:38.882
It looks like Indigenous Guardians.

00:03:39.674 --> 00:03:42.177
So, you've heard
of the "Guardians of the Galaxy?"

00:03:43.595 --> 00:03:48.558
Well, these guardians are doing a much
more important job right here on Earth,

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:50.435
just without the soundtrack.

00:03:50.477 --> 00:03:52.353
(Laughter)

00:03:53.062 --> 00:03:57.484
Guardians are trained experts who work
on behalf of their Indigenous nations.

00:03:57.984 --> 00:04:00.612
They're our eyes and ears on the land.

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:02.906
They monitor water quality,

00:04:02.906 --> 00:04:06.117
care for Indigenous-protected
and conserved areas,

00:04:06.159 --> 00:04:08.620
conduct research on climate impacts

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:12.791
and help restore species
like caribou, salmon and moose.

00:04:13.208 --> 00:04:17.420
Their work is rooted in Indigenous
and Western sciences

00:04:17.462 --> 00:04:20.715
and their training includes
everything from GIS mapping

00:04:20.715 --> 00:04:24.010
to spending time with elders
and knowledge keepers.

00:04:24.761 --> 00:04:27.138
We need this now more than ever.

00:04:28.223 --> 00:04:31.351
Guardians also do something more personal.

00:04:31.392 --> 00:04:32.644
Time and again,

00:04:32.685 --> 00:04:36.523
I've heard people say that being
a guardian has changed their lives.

00:04:37.023 --> 00:04:38.775
I think of the young Jarett Quock,

00:04:38.817 --> 00:04:40.860
a young man from the Tahltan First Nation

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:43.363
in what is now known as British Columbia.

00:04:43.363 --> 00:04:45.573
Jarett used to be
a heavy-equipment operator

00:04:45.573 --> 00:04:48.409
working on job sites
far from his community.

00:04:48.785 --> 00:04:51.371
He faced racism
from non-Indigenous peoples

00:04:51.371 --> 00:04:53.623
and non-unrelated,

00:04:53.665 --> 00:04:56.125
unfortunately, he struggled
with addictions.

00:04:56.167 --> 00:04:59.170
So when a job opened up
with a Tahltan Wildlife Guardians,

00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:00.505
he decided to take it.

00:05:01.005 --> 00:05:02.423
He said,

00:05:02.423 --> 00:05:06.302
"Being a guardian helped get me
through the tough times in my life.

00:05:07.887 --> 00:05:11.808
Being connected to the land
and talking with elders

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:13.893
helped me overcome my addictions.

00:05:14.394 --> 00:05:15.895
It brought pride to me.

00:05:16.521 --> 00:05:19.732
And at the end of the day,
I could walk away with the pride

00:05:19.774 --> 00:05:21.317
of being First Nations."

00:05:23.069 --> 00:05:27.949
(Applause)

00:05:28.241 --> 00:05:29.742
I know what he means.

00:05:29.784 --> 00:05:32.203
As a person who's been a witness to

00:05:32.203 --> 00:05:36.541
and felt the intergenerational trauma
from the colonial experience

00:05:36.583 --> 00:05:39.711
and the horrors of residential
schools or institutions,

00:05:39.752 --> 00:05:43.089
where Indigenous children were forcibly
removed from their homes

00:05:43.131 --> 00:05:45.925
and sent to so-called "schools"

00:05:45.925 --> 00:05:49.095
to be indoctrinated
into the dominant Canadian society,

00:05:49.095 --> 00:05:51.598
I've found no better strategy to healing

00:05:51.639 --> 00:05:54.601
than nurturing our
relationship with our place.

00:05:54.934 --> 00:05:59.397
I've seen the healing powers
of the land in action countless times.

00:05:59.772 --> 00:06:01.274
The land heals

00:06:01.274 --> 00:06:07.030
and I wish that experience for anyone
who has experienced and lives with trauma.

00:06:08.364 --> 00:06:11.451
Ethel Blondin-Andrew,
the leader I mentioned before,

00:06:11.492 --> 00:06:15.496
runs training camps for guardians
in the Northwest Territories.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:18.166
She says,

00:06:18.207 --> 00:06:22.420
"Residential schools cause great grief
and intergenerational trauma

00:06:22.462 --> 00:06:25.465
because they taught us
that we are not worthy of love."

00:06:26.424 --> 00:06:28.009
But we are worthy of love.

00:06:28.051 --> 00:06:29.928
The kids in our camps get it.

00:06:30.261 --> 00:06:34.891
They know that being on the land
is where they are the best human beings

00:06:34.933 --> 00:06:36.267
that they can be.

00:06:37.185 --> 00:06:40.355
Researchers have documented
the impacts of guardian programs.

00:06:40.730 --> 00:06:44.859
They can reduce incarceration
and increase health and well-being.

00:06:45.193 --> 00:06:49.614
People's health improves on the land
because they're on the land,

00:06:49.656 --> 00:06:51.616
because they're physically active

00:06:51.658 --> 00:06:53.826
and because they're happy.

00:06:54.619 --> 00:06:56.996
And guardians gained increased skills,

00:06:57.038 --> 00:07:00.667
higher incomes and pride
in cultural knowledge.

00:07:01.376 --> 00:07:06.047
In fact, one study in 2016 documented
the impacts of those guardian programs

00:07:06.089 --> 00:07:09.759
and showed that for every dollar invested,
there's a return on that investment

00:07:09.801 --> 00:07:14.764
of 2.5 dollars in social, economic
and environmental benefits.

00:07:15.264 --> 00:07:17.600
With sustained funding,

00:07:17.642 --> 00:07:21.646
that return on investment
jumps to almost four dollars.

00:07:22.605 --> 00:07:27.318
That's why that I believe that Indigenous
programs could do more for healing

00:07:27.318 --> 00:07:32.281
from the impacts of colonialism
than any other individual program.

00:07:34.283 --> 00:07:37.662
Guardians help honor
our responsibility of the land

00:07:37.662 --> 00:07:40.707
and they can create
a better future for all.

00:07:41.165 --> 00:07:44.335
Because guardianship
isn't just good for guardians,

00:07:44.335 --> 00:07:46.129
it's good for everyone

00:07:46.129 --> 00:07:49.257
because the land
is taking care of guardians

00:07:49.298 --> 00:07:51.718
and guardians are taking care of the land.

00:07:53.136 --> 00:07:56.347
Guardians help care
for some of the healthiest,

00:07:56.389 --> 00:07:58.725
most vibrant lands on the continent.

00:07:59.517 --> 00:08:01.060
Many work in the boreal forest

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:03.438
which stretches from Alaska
to Newfoundland.

00:08:03.813 --> 00:08:08.151
It is one of the largest intact
forests left on the planet.

00:08:09.068 --> 00:08:13.072
Having guardians on the ground
will help us sustain so many species

00:08:13.114 --> 00:08:18.036
like caribou, salmon, moose,
wolverine, lynx, songbirds,

00:08:18.036 --> 00:08:21.039
medicinal plants
and countless other species --

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:24.917
species that are unfortunately threatened
in much of the rest of the world.

00:08:26.210 --> 00:08:29.797
They also help protect some
of the largest protected areas

00:08:29.839 --> 00:08:31.174
on the planet.

00:08:31.591 --> 00:08:33.092
You may not know this,

00:08:33.092 --> 00:08:37.096
but some of the biggest, most ambitious
plans to protect areas in Canada

00:08:37.138 --> 00:08:39.640
are led by Indigenous peoples.

00:08:40.183 --> 00:08:47.106
(Applause)

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:52.570
Many of them, they're creating Indigenous
protected and conserved areas.

00:08:52.612 --> 00:08:56.449
These areas that they create based
on their own laws and cultures

00:08:56.491 --> 00:08:58.993
and often in partnerships
with Crown governments,

00:08:59.035 --> 00:09:01.746
or Canadian governments,
for the Americans in the room.

00:09:04.499 --> 00:09:06.709
You know, in fact,

00:09:06.709 --> 00:09:09.462
three of these areas alone
in the Northwest Territories

00:09:09.504 --> 00:09:13.007
span 50,000 square kilometers in size.

00:09:13.674 --> 00:09:15.718
That's about the size of Costa Rica.

00:09:16.803 --> 00:09:18.805
The Kaska Dena
in northern British Columbia

00:09:18.846 --> 00:09:21.891
are planning to create
a protected area the size of Switzerland.

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:23.851
And in northern Manitoba,

00:09:23.893 --> 00:09:26.229
four Dene and Cree nations
are coming together

00:09:26.229 --> 00:09:28.898
to protect the Seal River Watershed,

00:09:28.898 --> 00:09:32.151
home to caribou, belugas, polar bears

00:09:32.193 --> 00:09:34.362
and thousands of songbirds.

00:09:35.238 --> 00:09:40.326
It will be nearly five times the size
of Yellowstone National Park.

00:09:40.743 --> 00:09:45.498
(Applause)

00:09:45.790 --> 00:09:48.709
There are dozens of protected areas
in the works right now,

00:09:48.751 --> 00:09:50.336
right across Canada.

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:55.133
Many of them will protect some
of the largest carbon storehouses

00:09:55.174 --> 00:09:57.885
of any terrestrial ecosystem
on the planet.

00:09:58.761 --> 00:10:02.140
Remember when I said that my homeland
is at its best when it was cold?

00:10:02.473 --> 00:10:06.185
Well, its ability to capture
and store carbon over centuries

00:10:06.185 --> 00:10:08.563
is insured by that cold,

00:10:08.563 --> 00:10:11.691
because when organic matter
and litter fall to the ground,

00:10:11.691 --> 00:10:15.695
it decomposes extremely slowly
in its very deep soils.

00:10:16.028 --> 00:10:19.991
In fact, the boreal forest holds
twice as much carbon

00:10:20.032 --> 00:10:23.077
as the world's tropical
forests per hectare.

00:10:25.621 --> 00:10:28.040
(Applause)

00:10:28.082 --> 00:10:29.375
Yeah, go boreal!

00:10:29.375 --> 00:10:30.918
(Laughter)

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:32.170
This is why I'm here.

00:10:32.211 --> 00:10:33.713
(Laughter)

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:37.216
That protected area in the Seal River
Watershed I mentioned,

00:10:37.216 --> 00:10:40.636
it holds 1.7 billion tons of carbon

00:10:40.678 --> 00:10:44.765
equivalent to eight years’ worth
of greenhouse-gas emissions alone --

00:10:44.807 --> 00:10:46.142
in Canada alone.

00:10:46.601 --> 00:10:50.521
Protecting the watershed will help
keep that carbon in place.

00:10:51.189 --> 00:10:54.609
These are the kind of lands
that guardians are caring for.

00:10:55.693 --> 00:10:58.237
They also help heal and restore places.

00:10:58.279 --> 00:11:00.281
The Innu guardians, for example,

00:11:00.281 --> 00:11:03.784
they help care for and monitor
the largest nickel mine in the world

00:11:03.826 --> 00:11:06.204
at Voisey's Bay in Labrador.

00:11:06.704 --> 00:11:09.290
Guardians help ensure
that when development occurs,

00:11:09.332 --> 00:11:13.336
it happens with the informed
consent of our nations.

00:11:13.711 --> 00:11:18.424
That projects do not have an adverse
effect on our rights and titles.

00:11:18.841 --> 00:11:23.137
And that our nations and communities
can benefit or maximize those benefits

00:11:23.137 --> 00:11:25.014
from those development activities.

00:11:26.015 --> 00:11:29.936
Whether they are monitoring
the largest nickel mine in the world

00:11:29.977 --> 00:11:32.980
or sustaining giant carbon storehouses,

00:11:33.022 --> 00:11:37.485
guardians are helping honor
our responsibility to the land.

00:11:37.985 --> 00:11:41.489
And the benefits,
they ripple far and wide.

00:11:41.530 --> 00:11:46.869
As Gloria Enzo, a Ni Hat’Ni Dene guardian
from the Northwest Territory says,

00:11:46.911 --> 00:11:49.080
"We are sustaining
our traditional territories

00:11:49.121 --> 00:11:50.748
not only for us,

00:11:50.790 --> 00:11:52.625
but for the whole world."

00:11:53.626 --> 00:11:57.129
By honoring and respecting
Indigenous-led approaches to the land,

00:11:57.129 --> 00:12:00.675
we can create a better future for all.

00:12:01.384 --> 00:12:04.512
You know, there's a role
for everyone in this model.

00:12:04.887 --> 00:12:10.309
Every person, family, community, nation
is essential to dealing with the crises

00:12:10.351 --> 00:12:13.813
that we're facing as peoples
and as a planet.

00:12:15.189 --> 00:12:17.441
All actions, big and small,

00:12:17.483 --> 00:12:20.444
that contribute to addressing
these challenges

00:12:20.444 --> 00:12:22.405
will help us get closer to that goal.

00:12:23.656 --> 00:12:25.074
In fact,

00:12:25.116 --> 00:12:28.369
I'd like to ask you to join
Indigenous peoples

00:12:28.411 --> 00:12:31.539
in helping protect and create
a better future,

00:12:31.580 --> 00:12:34.542
one where we are ensuring
our collective futures

00:12:34.542 --> 00:12:37.336
as peoples on this planet.

00:12:39.922 --> 00:12:42.717
There is so much that we can do together.

00:12:43.134 --> 00:12:44.677
Specifically,

00:12:44.719 --> 00:12:48.264
study the history of Indigenous nations
with traditional territories

00:12:48.306 --> 00:12:50.182
in the places where you live and work.

00:12:50.891 --> 00:12:55.855
Talk with non-Indigenous friends
about Indigenous leadership on the land.

00:12:55.855 --> 00:12:59.734
Create space for Indigenous
voices and uplift them.

00:13:00.943 --> 00:13:05.239
Hold up our communities
and respect our knowledge systems.

00:13:06.824 --> 00:13:10.786
Make sure that you are using
your political voices

00:13:10.828 --> 00:13:14.332
and voting for leaders
who support this vision,

00:13:14.373 --> 00:13:17.084
because Indigenous guardians can ensure

00:13:17.084 --> 00:13:23.090
that we all have the future on this planet
that we deserve and want,

00:13:23.132 --> 00:13:27.053
so that we can all continue to have
an evolving love story

00:13:27.053 --> 00:13:29.805
with our lands, with our waters
that we call home.

00:13:30.681 --> 00:13:32.558
We can all heal the planet

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:36.854
by drawing on the knowledge
of our ancestors

00:13:36.896 --> 00:13:40.775
and blending them with the best tools
of the modern world.

00:13:41.776 --> 00:13:43.819
If we take care of the land,

00:13:43.819 --> 00:13:47.114
the land will take care of us forever.

00:13:48.449 --> 00:13:49.909
(In Ilnu-aimun: Thank you).

00:13:49.950 --> 00:13:56.374
(Applause)

